  "updated": "2026-08-10T06:44:22.181591+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA weak wave will move into western NY and PA early this morning and \neventually into central NY and northeast PA by day break. Sky cover \nwill begin to increase just ahead of this system with isolated \nshowers expected during the morning hours. All of this should limit \nany instability, so while thunderstorms cannot be ruled out, they \nshould remain sub-severe. After this moves through, there will be \nsome breaks in cloud cover and conditions will become warm and humid \nby the afternoon. Instability will climb to 1000 to 2000 J/kg of \nMLCAPE and 0-6 km shear will be 30 to 40 kts, though some model \nsolutions are showing lower shear values. This will occur ahead of \nremnants of a MCS that moves into the region this afternoon with \nisolated to scattered thunderstorms. Given the enhanced instability \nand shear values, there will be potential for strong to severe \nthunderstorms later today and into the evening hours. The main \nhazard would be gusty winds. Some model soundings show CAPE through \nthe hail growth zone and while lapse rates are not particularly \nsteep, hail will also be possible. Low-level shear looks to be too \nweak for any spin ups. PWATs will be around 1.75 inches though \nstorms should move along today. Still, one dry day likely has not \nbeen enough to dry out the ground, so localized flash flooding \ncannot be ruled out. \n\nFor this system, PoPs were capped at 70% as there is uncertainty if \nthe line will hold together as it moves through or be more broken. \nThe CAMs are split on this as coverage looks to be more scattered. \nThere is the chance that some areas do not see a drop of rain\ntoday. PoPs were also increased across northeast PA as guidance\nkept this region dry this morning, though if the line currently\nin western PA holds, rain is to be expected here.\n\nPrior to the showers and storms moving through, temperatures will \nrise into the upper 70s to mid 80s. It will be humid though as dew \npoints will be in the upper 60s to low 70s. Overnight, temperatures \nwill be fairly mild, only falling into the 60s. With calming winds, \nbreaks in cloud cover, and added moisture from today's showers, \npatchy fog will be possible in the valleys.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA ridge out west and broad troughing in the east will favor a fairly \nactive pattern this week. Ridge riders will pass over the Great \nLakes Region and pass near or over our region. Weak high pressure \nand drier air will build into the region the night prior though a \nweak system will likely clip the Southern Tier and northeast PA on \nTuesday with passing showers and thunderstorms. NBM guidance was not \npicking up on this, possibly lagging behind newer guidance, so \nConsall was blended in to bump up PoPs. Wednesday is expected to be \nmostly dry as well\n\nLate in the week, the upper level trough begins to dig slightly and \nleads to the next system moving in Wednesday night into Thursday. \nHigh pressure then follows to bring another dry period late in the \nweek and to start the weekend. A weak low pressure then moves \nthrough Sunday with another batch of showers that may linger into \nthe start of next week. \n\nThere is quite bit of uncertainty with each system mentioned above, \nespecially late in the week and over the weekend. Models continue to \ndiffer on exact tracks and timing, which impacts the coverage. \nThere is confidence that there will be dry periods and not any\nparticular day is expected to be a wash out.\n\nTemperatures will trend cooler during the later half of the week. \nHighs will be in the 70s to low 80s. Temperatures overnight will \nfall into the 50s.",
